chat_messages.text:
 For distinguishing these three cases:

```typescript
function isNull(value: null | Type<object> | (() => object)): value is null {
  return value === null;
}

function isConstructor(value: Type<object> | (() => object)): value is Type<object> {
  return value.prototype?.constructor === value && value.prototype !== Function.prototype;
}
```

The tricky part is `Type<object>` vs `() => object` since both are functions at runtime. The reliable signal is `prototype.constructor` — class constructors (and old-school `function` constructors) have a `.prototype` with a `.constructor` back-reference, while arrow functions have no `.prototype` at all.

So the full narrowing flow:

```typescript
declare const value: null | Type<object> | (() => object);

if (value === null) {
  // null
} else if (value.prototype?.constructor === value) {
  // Type<object> — has a prototype chain
} else {
  // () => object — arrow function or plain function without prototype
}
```

**Caveats:**
- A `function` declaration (not arrow) used as `() => object` would also have `.prototype`, so this only works reliably if your factory functions are arrows.
- Class constructors written as `class Foo {}` are correctly caught since `Foo.prototype.constructor === Foo`.
- If you can control the shape, adding a discriminant like a `[Symbol.hasInstance]` or a static marker property is more robust than prototype inspection.

